@page "/framework/built-on-it"

<PageTitle>Built on it · louis-agent</PageTitle>
<PageHead Description="Three agents run on the louis-agent engine today: Puls, the coding agent and the guide, and the open packages the framework stands on." />

<Band Id="top" Kicker="Built on it">
    <h2 class="chrome">Three agents run on the engine today.</h2>
    <GraphView Spec="DemosGraph.Spec" Title="The framework, and what is built on it" />
    <p class="lede">Each is a demo, the way your own project would be: its own program, its own identity, and the packs it names.</p>
    <CardDeck>
        <Card Title="Puls" Summary="A general assistant in everyday South African Afrikaans, English too. It hands each task to a specialist and shows the work live.">
            <p>It doesn't answer with tools itself: it hands each task to a specialist, a second session with only the tools that task needs and a role that says how to work and report back. The work shows live, with the sources it read and what it cost, in rand.</p>
            <p>Five specialists today: a researcher, a reader, a historian, an analyst and a cook. Its packs find a shop's branches near you, estimate prices from Stats SA's public averages, and read recipes from South African sites.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="The coding agent" Summary="Works on your repository with real tools, from Rider, the web, the terminal or the API. Pushing stays with you.">
            <p>All of its tools are packs: files, git, dotnet, Python, PowerShell, bash and web search. It runs inside Rider through the Agent Client Protocol, in a web app with a changes view and a branches view, in the terminal, over the HTTP API, and its tools alone over MCP. Pushing always stays with you.</p>
            <p>A proof of concept beside it: an orchestrator that reads a service's errors and has the coding agent fix and verify them.</p>
        </Card>
        <Card Title="The guide" Summary="The guide in this site's corner. It answers from the framework's own docs and sells what the site sells.">
            <p>It answers about the framework from the framework's own docs, read-only, reads before it speaks, and sells what the site sells: a hosted website with an agent like itself, and custom agents. It speaks plainly to a visitor and technically to a developer.</p>
            <p>One voice on three packs: the docs it reads, a lead it sends Louis when a visitor says yes, and a way to take the visitor to a page. It runs on the site's own host, with nothing else loaded. A fourth, a team that triages a service's errors and fixes them, is being built on the same engine.</p>
        </Card>
    </CardDeck>
</Band>

<Band Id="stands-on" Kicker="What it stands on" Class="stands-on">
    <h3>Microsoft's own pieces, and a few open ones. Nothing paid.</h3>
    <p class="lede">The framework is 100% C# on .NET 10, and it adopts before it builds: a maintained package under a permissive licence wins over code of our own. These are the ones it stands on, each with its licence.</p>
    <ul class="rules">
        <li><strong><code>Microsoft.Extensions.AI</code></strong> One chat client across providers, and the function-calling loop. MIT.</li>
        <li><strong><code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.WebAssembly</code></strong> Blazor WebAssembly: every site, this one included, is C# in the browser. MIT, part of ASP.NET Core.</li>
        <li><strong><code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore</code></strong> Sign-in, registration, passkeys and a second factor, with the tenant added by us. MIT.</li>
        <li><strong><code>Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer</code></strong> The accounts' store, on SQL Server or Azure SQL. MIT.</li>
        <li><strong><code>MongoDB.Driver</code></strong> The eval runs, the recipe library and the price tables. Apache 2.0.</li>
        <li><strong><code>OpenTelemetry</code></strong> Every request traced, exported to Application Insights or any collector. Apache 2.0.</li>
        <li><strong><code>ModelContextProtocol</code></strong> The MCP server that hands the tools to another client. MIT.</li>
        <li><strong><code>OllamaSharp</code></strong> A local model through Ollama. MIT.</li>
        <li><strong><code>PuppeteerSharp</code></strong> The headless browser that draws a page a reader can't. MIT.</li>
        <li><strong><code>AspNetCore.SassCompiler</code></strong> The stylesheets, compiled at build. MIT.</li>
    </ul>
</Band>

<Band Id="hosts" Kicker="Hosts">
    <p class="lede">The framework's hosts are libraries. A demo's few lines start the ones it wants.</p>
    <div class="grid-3">
        <div class="panel small"><h4>Rider</h4><p>An Agent Client Protocol server over stdio for Rider's AI chat.</p></div>
        <div class="panel small"><h4>Web app and HTTP API</h4><p>Sessions with answers streamed as server-sent events. The API serves the web app.</p></div>
        <div class="panel small"><h4>CLI and MCP server</h4><p>Interactive or one-shot prompts in the terminal, and the tools handed to any MCP client, which brings its own model.</p></div>
    </div>
</Band>
